00:13:14,450 --> 00:13:16,200
Generally, we're going
to change refitting

250
00:13:16,200 --> 00:13:18,040
the baseline each time.

251
00:13:18,040 --> 00:13:20,790
So we can re estimate
the baseline.

252
00:13:20,790 --> 00:13:24,190
Again, it doesn't matter what
we pick for the baseline.

253
00:13:24,190 --> 00:13:25,980
It will always be
unbiased, but there will

254
00:13:25,980 --> 00:13:27,640
be better or worse choices.

255
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:29,830
So you can imagine,
if the baseline is 0,

256
00:13:29,830 --> 00:13:31,670
it will never make
any difference.

257
00:13:31,670 --> 00:13:33,420
The goal is to,
hopefully, have a baseline

258
00:13:33,420 --> 00:13:35,970
that's pretty informative
and has a value close

259
00:13:35,970 --> 00:13:38,850
to the value of your policy.

260
00:13:38,850 --> 00:13:41,550
And so then we'll update the
policy using our policy gradient

261
00:13:41,550 --> 00:13:45,270
estimate, which is a
sum of these terms.

262
00:13:45,270 --> 00:13:50,430
So we're going to use all
of these terms, where we've

263
00:13:50,430 --> 00:13:53,280
got that derivative with
respect to the log of the policy

264
00:13:53,280 --> 00:13:58,260
parameters times our
advantage, and then repeat.

265
00:13:58,260 --> 00:14:02,230
So this is a vanilla
policy gradient algorithm.

266
00:14:02,230 --> 00:14:03,640
Yeah.

267
00:14:03,640 --> 00:14:06,910
There is no discount
factor in the return.

268
00:14:06,910 --> 00:14:08,410
Is that intentional, or what?

269
00:14:08,410 --> 00:14:09,860
Yeah, good question.

270
00:14:09,860 --> 00:14:12,700
So right now, the question is,
there's no discount factor.

271
00:14:12,700 --> 00:14:14,200
There's no discount
factor right now

272
00:14:14,200 --> 00:14:16,533
because we're assuming we're
in the fully episodic case,

273
00:14:16,533 --> 00:14:18,350
so we don't have to
have a discount factor.

274
00:14:18,350 --> 00:14:20,830
You could certainly
include one if you want to.

275
00:14:20,830 --> 00:14:24,400
Yeah, so for right now here, we
don't have a discount factor.

276
00:14:24,400 --> 00:14:25,930
Now, one thing that
you might think

277
00:14:25,930 --> 00:14:27,250
about when you're
starting to look at this

278
00:14:27,250 --> 00:14:29,740
is to say, well, a lot of this
feels like the Monte Carlo

279
00:14:29,740 --> 00:14:32,600
estimation that we did
earlier in the class.

280
00:14:32,600 --> 00:14:38,230
We've been using these G
estimators, this return,

281
00:14:38,230 --> 00:14:39,850
to estimate what
is the performance

282
00:14:39,850 --> 00:14:43,060
of the policy from this time
step to the end of the episode.

283
00:14:43,060 --> 00:14:45,680
But as you might
imagine in this case,

284
00:14:45,680 --> 00:14:48,580
that generally is a
pretty noisy estimate.

285
00:14:48,580 --> 00:14:51,915
So then the question is going
to be, could we maybe do better.

286
00:14:55,940 --> 00:14:58,262
So there's two places
we could imagine.

287
00:14:58,262 --> 00:15:00,470
I guess there's two things
here that we could imagine

288
00:15:00,470 --> 00:15:02,130
plugging in other choices for.

289
00:15:02,130 --> 00:15:05,930
There is what is the
return of the policy

290
00:15:05,930 --> 00:15:08,490
from a particular action
till the end of the episode,

291
00:15:08,490 --> 00:15:11,480
and what is my general
estimate of the performance

292
00:15:11,480 --> 00:15:12,130
in that state.

293
00:15:14,750 --> 00:15:16,400
So one thing you
can imagine here

294
00:15:16,400 --> 00:15:19,230
is, if we think back to Q
functions and value functions,

295
00:15:19,230 --> 00:15:21,770
maybe we could plug those in,
instead of using the return

296
00:15:21,770 --> 00:15:24,020
and using a generic baseline.

297
00:15:24,020 --> 00:15:26,533
So you could plug in--
instead of saying,

298
00:15:26,533 --> 00:15:27,950
what is the return
from this state

299
00:15:27,950 --> 00:15:29,492
and action to the
end of the episode,

300
00:15:29,492 --> 00:15:31,370
you could imagine
plugging in the Q

301
00:15:31,370 --> 00:15:34,460
value for the current policy
from the state and action

302
00:15:34,460 --> 00:15:35,580
to the end of the episode.

303
00:15:35,580 --> 00:15:37,710
And we can either make
gamma equal to 1 or not.

304
00:15:37,710 --> 00:15:42,920
We're going to generally
assume no for now.

305
00:15:42,920 --> 00:15:47,340
Assume episodic.

306
00:15:47,340 --> 00:15:53,560
So you can set gamma equal to 1.

307
00:15:53,560 --> 00:15:56,840
And the state value function
could be a good baseline.

308
00:16:00,330 --> 00:16:02,600
And just to remember
here, on this slide,

309
00:16:02,600 --> 00:16:04,850
you can think of G as kind
of being like a Q function,

310
00:16:04,850 --> 00:16:06,330
and V as being a value function.

311
00:16:06,330 --> 00:16:10,720
So this would be an
alternative we could do.

312
00:16:10,720 --> 00:16:14,922
OK, so let's think about how we
generally could reduce variance.

313
00:16:14,922 --> 00:16:16,630
So what we've seen so
far is we're mostly

314
00:16:16,630 --> 00:16:18,280
using Monte Carlo like returns.

315
00:16:18,280 --> 00:16:20,155
Now, let's see if we
can do something better.

316
00:16:22,600 --> 00:16:25,000
So one thing we
can do now is we're

317
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:28,730
going to try to plug in and use
things like state action values.

318
00:16:28,730 --> 00:16:31,060
And this is where the idea
of actor critic methods

319
00:16:31,060 --> 00:16:33,520
come in, which are also really
popular in reinforcement

320
00:16:33,520 --> 00:16:35,760
learning.

321
00:16:35,760 --> 00:16:39,420
So the idea here
is that we could

322
00:16:39,420 --> 00:16:41,490
reduce the variance of
this estimate of the value

323
00:16:41,490 --> 00:16:44,640
function at a single st
from a single rollout

324
00:16:44,640 --> 00:16:48,390
by bootstrapping or doing
function approximation

325
00:16:48,390 --> 00:16:49,770
at that point.

326
00:16:49,770 --> 00:16:52,560
So you could think back to
deep Q-learning or something

327
00:16:52,560 --> 00:16:55,110
like that as a way for us to
approximate what the value might

328
00:16:55,110 --> 00:17:01,450
be, or just general sort of deep
learning for the value function.

329
00:17:01,450 --> 00:17:03,700
So when we do this,
we end up with what

330
00:17:03,700 --> 00:17:05,480
is called actor critic methods.

331
00:17:05,480 --> 00:17:08,890
The idea is that the
actor is the policy.

332
00:17:08,890 --> 00:17:17,750
So the actor is the policy
often parameterized by theta.

333
00:17:17,750 --> 00:17:21,349
And the value function, or the
state action value function,

334
00:17:21,349 --> 00:17:23,630
is the critic.

335
00:17:23,630 --> 00:17:27,040
And it's representing
a V or a Q function.

336
00:17:27,040 --> 00:17:28,790
So that's why they're
called actor critic.

337
00:17:28,790 --> 00:17:31,250
Actor is our policy
parameterization.

338
00:17:31,250 --> 00:17:33,380
Critic is our
state action value.

339
00:17:33,380 --> 00:17:35,360
And the great thing
is that we can

340
00:17:35,360 --> 00:17:39,440
use both of those inside of
a policy gradient algorithm.

341
00:17:39,440 --> 00:17:43,100
So you are constantly updating
an estimate of the state action

342
00:17:43,100 --> 00:17:46,010
value, as well as having
an explicit policy

343
00:17:46,010 --> 00:17:46,940
parameterization.

344
00:17:46,940 --> 00:17:49,910
And you use them together to,
hopefully, increase the rate

345
00:17:49,910 --> 00:17:52,278
at which we learn to
get a good policy.

346
00:17:52,278 --> 00:17:54,320
Now, in this case, normally
what we're doing here

347
00:17:54,320 --> 00:17:56,850
is we're gathering
data using the policy.

348
00:17:56,850 --> 00:18:01,663
And then we're using that
data to fit a critic.

349
00:18:01,663 --> 00:18:03,080
And the reason we
call it a critic

350
00:18:03,080 --> 00:18:04,910
is because the critic
is sort of trying

351
00:18:04,910 --> 00:18:09,440
to estimate the performance
of an explicit representation

352
00:18:09,440 --> 00:18:11,690
of the performance
of the policy.

353
00:18:11,690 --> 00:18:13,820
So the actor makes
decisions, and the critic

354
00:18:13,820 --> 00:18:15,370
says that's how good it was.

355
00:18:15,370 --> 00:18:17,430
So that's why it's
called actor critic.

356
00:18:17,430 --> 00:18:20,570
A3C is a pretty popular
actor critic method.

357
00:18:20,570 --> 00:18:21,820
There's quite a lot of others.

358
00:18:21,820 --> 00:18:23,940
So many of the reinforcement
learning algorithms

359
00:18:23,940 --> 00:18:26,470
will end up being essentially
actor critic algorithms.

360
00:18:26,470 --> 00:18:28,637
And so it'll be useful to
have both representations.

361
00:18:28,637 --> 00:18:30,012
So if you think
of it, you'd have

362
00:18:30,012 --> 00:18:32,490
a sort of a deep neural network
to represent your policy,

363
00:18:32,490 --> 00:18:35,118
and you'd have a deep neural
network, a separate one-- you

364
00:18:35,118 --> 00:18:36,660
could share parameters,
but you don't

365
00:18:36,660 --> 00:18:40,230
have to-- to represent
your value function.

366
00:18:40,230 --> 00:18:40,730
All right.

367
00:18:40,730 --> 00:18:45,520
Once we do that, we can think
of rewriting our policy gradient

368
00:18:45,520 --> 00:18:46,400
formulas.

369
00:18:46,400 --> 00:18:49,130
So this was what we had before.

370
00:18:49,130 --> 00:18:52,340
We could approximate
this now as saying, well,

371
00:18:52,340 --> 00:18:55,360
what if we just plugged in
instead of that return g, which

372
00:18:55,360 --> 00:18:58,720
is that sum over the rewards,
we plugged in a Q function,

373
00:18:58,720 --> 00:19:01,120
and we plugged in a
parameterized Q function

374
00:19:01,120 --> 00:19:02,530
with a parameter w.

375
00:19:02,530 --> 00:19:05,020
So these were our weights.

376
00:19:05,020 --> 00:19:06,660
So now, just to
highlight here, we're

377
00:19:06,660 --> 00:19:10,150
going to have these two sets
of parameters w and theta,

378
00:19:10,150 --> 00:19:14,200
theta for the policy, w
for the value function.

379
00:19:14,200 --> 00:19:17,780
And if we let the baseline
be an estimate of the V,

380
00:19:17,780 --> 00:19:21,400
then we can just directly write
down a state action advantage

381
00:19:21,400 --> 00:19:25,090
function, where we
look at the difference

382
00:19:25,090 --> 00:19:29,902
between the Q and the V. So now
V is serving as our baseline.

383
00:19:29,902 --> 00:19:32,110
And I'll just highlight here
that using the advantage

384
00:19:32,110 --> 00:19:35,440
function was one of
the first things that--

385
00:19:35,440 --> 00:19:38,420
I got best paper maybe in 2016.

386
00:19:38,420 --> 00:19:41,000
Right after deep Q-learning
had started coming out

387
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,652
when people thought about
these different adaptations,

388
00:19:43,652 --> 00:19:45,110
one of the things
that was proposed

389
00:19:45,110 --> 00:19:46,940
is to think about
trying to maximize

390
00:19:46,940 --> 00:19:49,700
with respect to advantages.

391
00:19:49,700 --> 00:19:52,220
But here, we're going to be
using this within a policy

392
00:19:52,220 --> 00:19:55,247
gradient approach.

393
00:19:55,247 --> 00:19:57,080
Now, one of the things
you might wonder here

394
00:19:57,080 --> 00:19:59,040
is, OK, well, we've
got these extremes.

395
00:19:59,040 --> 00:20:01,730
On the one hand, you could
have this Monte Carlo return

396
00:20:01,730 --> 00:20:03,882
of what is the value
of the state in action

397
00:20:03,882 --> 00:20:05,840
that you get from starting
that state in action

398
00:20:05,840 --> 00:20:07,800
and rolling out to the
end of the episode.

399
00:20:07,800 --> 00:20:10,550
And the other is you could
plug in a Q function.

400
00:20:10,550 --> 00:20:15,140
Now, there might be some sort
of blending between these two.

401
00:20:15,140 --> 00:20:18,620
So these are known
as n step estimators.

402
00:20:18,620 --> 00:20:20,360
So a critic, in
general, doesn't have

403
00:20:20,360 --> 00:20:21,987
to pick sort of a
temporal difference.

404
00:20:21,987 --> 00:20:24,320
Temporal difference in the
way that we've seen it so far

405
00:20:24,320 --> 00:20:26,840
is normally-- so I'll
just write down here.

406
00:20:26,840 --> 00:20:31,040
We've seen it as
TD0, which means

407
00:20:31,040 --> 00:20:35,840
we have the immediate reward
plus gamma times V of s prime.

408
00:20:35,840 --> 00:20:39,230
So in TD0, you take you think
of your immediate reward,

409
00:20:39,230 --> 00:20:40,850
and then you plug
in or bootstrap

410
00:20:40,850 --> 00:20:43,580
immediately when you say
ns on the next state.

411
00:20:43,580 --> 00:20:46,330
And I'm going to plug-in my
value for that next state.

412
00:20:46,330 --> 00:20:48,330
So if you think back to
our tree representation,

413
00:20:48,330 --> 00:20:50,600
it's like you see one
actual observed return,

414
00:20:50,600 --> 00:20:52,940
and then you plug-in
your estimate.

415
00:20:52,940 --> 00:20:56,510
But in general, you could trade
off between taking one step

416
00:20:56,510 --> 00:20:58,130
or taking two steps,
or three steps,

417
00:20:58,130 --> 00:21:02,180
and then plugging in your
estimate of the return.

418
00:21:02,180 --> 00:21:05,450
So in particular,
here's a number

419
00:21:05,450 --> 00:21:09,260
of different types of
estimators you could look at.

420
00:21:09,260 --> 00:21:13,280
So let's call this
r hat 1, which

421
00:21:13,280 --> 00:21:15,128
is get your immediate reward.

422
00:21:15,128 --> 00:21:16,920
This is what we've seen
before, plus gamma.

423
00:21:16,920 --> 00:21:18,410
Then you bootstrap.

424
00:21:18,410 --> 00:21:21,470
A second one would be
you take your next 2.

425
00:21:21,470 --> 00:21:25,430
Again, your gamma can be 1 or
not, and then you plug in it.

426
00:21:25,430 --> 00:21:29,120
And sort of r hat infinity
would be your normal Monte Carlo

427
00:21:29,120 --> 00:21:31,620
return, which is you don't
do any bootstrapping.

428
00:21:31,620 --> 00:21:34,100
You just sum up
all your rewards.

429
00:21:34,100 --> 00:21:35,750
And you can think
of each of these

430
00:21:35,750 --> 00:21:38,040
as being estimates
of your Q function.

431
00:21:38,040 --> 00:21:40,820
And then you could just
get advantage estimators

432
00:21:40,820 --> 00:21:43,970
where you subtract off the V
of your current state in each

433
00:21:43,970 --> 00:21:46,280
of those settings.

434
00:21:46,280 --> 00:21:47,970
So those are all
things you could do.

435
00:21:47,970 --> 00:21:49,650
They're called n
step estimators,

436
00:21:49,650 --> 00:21:53,123
where n is the number of time
steps until you bootstrap.

437
00:21:53,123 --> 00:21:55,040
And one of the important
things to think about

438
00:21:55,040 --> 00:21:58,640
is where you might want to
trade off between these ones.

439
00:21:58,640 --> 00:22:00,900
So we'll do just a Check
Your Understanding now.

440
00:22:00,900 --> 00:22:02,720
If we think about
introducing these type

441
00:22:02,720 --> 00:22:09,900
of blended estimators, how does
bias and variance trade off?

442
00:22:09,900 --> 00:22:12,160
So why don't we go
ahead and do that now.

443
00:23:13,323 --> 00:23:14,740
Can you select
like more than one?

444
00:23:14,740 --> 00:23:15,965
You should be able to.

445
00:23:15,965 --> 00:23:16,590
Does that work?

446
00:23:31,725 --> 00:23:33,850
OK, I'll give you one more
minute to think about it

447
00:23:33,850 --> 00:23:35,210
and put it in your answer.

448
00:23:35,210 --> 00:23:38,560
And then there's a
lot of variability

449
00:23:38,560 --> 00:23:39,940
in what people are saying.

450
00:23:39,940 --> 00:23:43,380
And so why don't you talk to
your neighbor in a second?

451
00:23:51,240 --> 00:23:51,740
All right.

452
00:23:51,740 --> 00:23:52,550
Turn to a neighbor.

453
00:23:52,550 --> 00:23:53,425
Compare what you got.

454
00:24:00,720 --> 00:24:04,640
[INAUDIBLE]

455
00:24:24,845 --> 00:24:25,970
What do you think about it?

456
00:24:25,970 --> 00:24:29,379
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

457
00:24:30,844 --> 00:24:31,344
Yeah?

458
00:24:35,770 --> 00:24:37,280
you are subtracting
the V function.

459
00:24:37,280 --> 00:24:39,820
But if you think back to
Monte Carlo and TD methods,

460
00:24:39,820 --> 00:24:41,450
did they have the same bias?

461
00:24:41,450 --> 00:24:43,960
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

462
00:24:52,320 --> 00:24:55,700
Well, this is the question.

463
00:24:55,700 --> 00:24:57,190
Do these both have
the same bias?

464
00:24:57,190 --> 00:25:00,090
So if you ignore subtracting
a hundred [INAUDIBLE]

465
00:25:00,090 --> 00:25:02,280
the first part of
the estimate, do you

466
00:25:02,280 --> 00:25:06,490
think the first one has
higher bias or the second one?

467
00:25:06,490 --> 00:25:09,415
Yeah, I don't know
why it has high bias.

468
00:25:09,415 --> 00:25:11,040
If you think back,
the first one should

469
00:25:11,040 --> 00:25:13,192
be like the temporal
difference method,

470
00:25:13,192 --> 00:25:14,900
and the second one
should be Monte Carlo.

471
00:25:17,432 --> 00:25:19,140
And remember, I guess,
it should be clear

472
00:25:19,140 --> 00:25:23,820
that these Vs are all estimates,
so they're not converged.

473
00:25:23,820 --> 00:25:28,240
So then the top one
has higher bias.

474
00:25:28,240 --> 00:25:28,740
Exactly.

475
00:25:28,740 --> 00:25:34,150
The second one has lower bias
because of the actual value.

476
00:25:34,150 --> 00:25:35,140
Exactly, yeah.

477
00:25:35,140 --> 00:25:38,850
And then what do you think
that means for the variance?

478
00:25:38,850 --> 00:25:45,090
I guess higher variance also for
the top one because, I guess,

479
00:25:45,090 --> 00:25:47,760
because it's an estimate.

480
00:25:47,760 --> 00:25:48,370
Close.

481
00:25:48,370 --> 00:25:49,980
Yeah, so for the
first one, you're

482
00:25:49,980 --> 00:25:51,690
totally right that it's got
higher bias because you're

483
00:25:51,690 --> 00:25:52,773
immediately bootstrapping.

484
00:25:52,773 --> 00:25:55,120
But in general, it will
have lower variance.

485
00:25:55,120 --> 00:25:55,620
OK.

486
00:25:55,620 --> 00:25:57,660
Is that just
generally a trade off?

487
00:25:57,660 --> 00:25:58,500
Yeah, yeah.

488
00:25:58,500 --> 00:25:59,990
Normally, the Monte
Carlo methods,

489
00:25:59,990 --> 00:26:01,615
because you're summing
them up, they're

490
00:26:01,615 --> 00:26:02,780
normally totally unbiased.

491
00:26:02,780 --> 00:26:04,020
But they have lots of terms.

492
00:26:04,020 --> 00:26:06,310
So you could think of there's
lots of stochasticity.

493
00:26:06,310 --> 00:26:06,920
OK.

494
00:26:06,920 --> 00:26:08,860
Yeah, and then the other
way is normally aggressive.

495
00:26:08,860 --> 00:26:09,360
I see.

496
00:26:09,360 --> 00:26:10,360
OK, that makes sense.

497
00:26:10,360 --> 00:26:11,091
Cool.

498
00:26:11,091 --> 00:26:14,780
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

499
00:26:21,830 --> 00:26:22,330
All right.

500
00:26:22,330 --> 00:26:24,423
I'm going to ask if people
all turn this-- yeah,

501
00:26:24,423 --> 00:26:26,340
I'm going to ask, did
it may change their mind

502
00:26:26,340 --> 00:26:28,580
after talking to someone?

503
00:26:28,580 --> 00:26:29,700
OK, at least a few.

504
00:26:29,700 --> 00:26:30,390
Yep, all right.

505
00:26:30,390 --> 00:26:33,540
So I think one of
the things, too,

506
00:26:33,540 --> 00:26:36,683
I just wanted to clarify here
is that I find it easiest to--

507
00:26:36,683 --> 00:26:38,600
I think it's often easiest
to think about this

508
00:26:38,600 --> 00:26:41,030
without subtracting
the V, because the V is

509
00:26:41,030 --> 00:26:42,168
the same in both of these.

510
00:26:42,168 --> 00:26:44,210
So you can just focus to
understand which of them

511
00:26:44,210 --> 00:26:46,800
has high variance or high bias.

512
00:26:46,800 --> 00:26:48,840
You can just focus
on the first things.

513
00:26:48,840 --> 00:26:50,730
And when you look at
just the first things,

514
00:26:50,730 --> 00:26:52,220
it should remind
you of Monte Carlo

515
00:26:52,220 --> 00:26:54,740
methods versus temporal
different methods.

516
00:26:54,740 --> 00:26:59,300
So the first one has low
variance and high bias.

517
00:26:59,300 --> 00:27:02,160
Does somebody want to say why?

518
00:27:02,160 --> 00:27:06,690
So the first one, which looks
kind of like a TD0 update,

519
00:27:06,690 --> 00:27:13,110
so this one has low variance.

520
00:27:13,110 --> 00:27:19,047
So a1, low variance, high bias.

521
00:27:19,047 --> 00:27:20,630
Does anyone want to
share why that is?

522
00:27:25,040 --> 00:27:25,880
Which is it?

523
00:27:25,880 --> 00:27:27,410
Sorry, it wasn't on top.

524
00:27:27,410 --> 00:27:28,250
It wasn't, no.

525
00:27:31,407 --> 00:27:33,990
Just to make sure, I'm going to
explain what each of them has.

526
00:27:33,990 --> 00:27:34,703
But yes.

527
00:27:34,703 --> 00:27:35,870
Do you want to say somewhat?

528
00:27:35,870 --> 00:27:36,370
Yeah.

529
00:27:36,370 --> 00:27:38,270
And remind me your name.

530
00:27:38,270 --> 00:27:40,590
I'm not entirely
sure, but I think

531
00:27:40,590 --> 00:27:42,840
that intuition, at least
when I was thinking about it,

532
00:27:42,840 --> 00:27:47,960
was that using the actual values
of, for example, rt plus 1

533
00:27:47,960 --> 00:27:52,280
is more accurate, versus if
you bootstrap very early,

534
00:27:52,280 --> 00:27:53,430
it's more of an estimate.

535
00:27:53,430 --> 00:27:54,870
It's not as accurate.

536
00:27:54,870 --> 00:27:56,010
Yeah, that's exactly right.

537
00:27:56,010 --> 00:27:58,370
So it's the same as in
temporal difference methods.

538
00:27:58,370 --> 00:28:00,485
In general, it's a
little misleading

539
00:28:00,485 --> 00:28:01,610
sometimes to look at these.

540
00:28:01,610 --> 00:28:03,652
Maybe I should put hats
over all these next year.

541
00:28:03,652 --> 00:28:05,790
But all of these Vs
are just estimates.

542
00:28:05,790 --> 00:28:08,600
They're given finite amounts of
data and however many backups

543
00:28:08,600 --> 00:28:09,800
we've done, et cetera.

544
00:28:09,800 --> 00:28:11,280
V is an approximation.

545
00:28:11,280 --> 00:28:13,570
So this is an estimate.

546
00:28:13,570 --> 00:28:15,460
So this isn't true.

547
00:28:15,460 --> 00:28:18,070
And if it's not true,
it's probably biased.

548
00:28:18,070 --> 00:28:22,700
So in general, V will not
be an unbiased estimator.

549
00:28:22,700 --> 00:28:27,730
And so this means that we're
only using 1.r from the true

550
00:28:27,730 --> 00:28:30,360
policy, and then we're
immediately bootstrapping.

551
00:28:30,360 --> 00:28:33,415
So in general, this is going
to be high bias or higher bias,

552
00:28:33,415 --> 00:28:35,540
but it's generally going
to be pretty low variance.

553
00:28:35,540 --> 00:28:39,250
And the way to think about this
is that each of the rewards,

554
00:28:39,250 --> 00:28:42,790
in general, are going to be
from a stochastic process

555
00:28:42,790 --> 00:28:44,613
because you're taking
a series of steps.

556
00:28:44,613 --> 00:28:46,030
And then at each
one, you're going

557
00:28:46,030 --> 00:28:48,260
to sample a reward from there.

558
00:28:48,260 --> 00:28:51,140
So you only have one
really random thing here,

559
00:28:51,140 --> 00:28:53,410
and then one thing
that is fixed.

560
00:28:53,410 --> 00:28:56,290
It might be wrong,
but it's fixed.

561
00:28:56,290 --> 00:29:00,850
In contrast, this one, which
looks like a Monte Carlo

562
00:29:00,850 --> 00:29:04,240
estimate, is going to have
high variance because it's

563
00:29:04,240 --> 00:29:06,820
got all of these
different rewards that

564
00:29:06,820 --> 00:29:10,040
are all being sampled
from a stochastic process.

565
00:29:10,040 --> 00:29:13,163
So if you think of
it this way, imagine

566
00:29:13,163 --> 00:29:15,830
it's something where your robot
can walk anywhere over the room.

567
00:29:15,830 --> 00:29:18,680
And under your
policy, your policy

568
00:29:18,680 --> 00:29:22,980
can go in all of these
different directions.

569
00:29:22,980 --> 00:29:26,170
But when you actually just
execute one trajectory,

570
00:29:26,170 --> 00:29:28,200
you're just going
to get one of those.

571
00:29:28,200 --> 00:29:30,940
And so its variance
generally is enormous.

572
00:29:30,940 --> 00:29:34,680
And this might be true
even if, on average,

573
00:29:34,680 --> 00:29:37,200
you kind of have a trajectory
like this or something.

574
00:29:37,200 --> 00:29:40,710
So in general, this one is going
to be really high variance,

575
00:29:40,710 --> 00:29:45,210
but it's generally going
to be low or 0 bias.

576
00:29:45,210 --> 00:29:46,960
Why is it low or 0 bias?

577
00:29:46,960 --> 00:29:50,520
Because this actually is
a return from the policy

578
00:29:50,520 --> 00:29:52,140
that you're executing.

579
00:29:52,140 --> 00:29:55,140
So in expectation,
this really is

580
00:29:55,140 --> 00:29:57,620
equal to the value
of that policy.

581
00:29:57,620 --> 00:30:00,990
So it's generally
low or 0 bias, but it

582
00:30:00,990 --> 00:30:02,430
can be really high variance.

583
00:30:02,430 --> 00:30:05,140
And so that should maybe
give some intuition over--

584
00:30:05,140 --> 00:30:06,930
and we were just
discussing this.

585
00:30:06,930 --> 00:30:08,763
In general, it's going
to be, unfortunately,

586
00:30:08,763 --> 00:30:11,350
be a trade off between
low variance and low bias.

587
00:30:11,350 --> 00:30:14,790
And so often you're going to
want things in terms of n.

588
00:30:14,790 --> 00:30:17,910
So if that's the n
step, and you want

589
00:30:17,910 --> 00:30:19,830
to minimize your
mean squared error,

590
00:30:19,830 --> 00:30:21,330
often you're going
to end up wanting

591
00:30:21,330 --> 00:30:23,350
to do something where
it's a couple steps,

592
00:30:23,350 --> 00:30:25,690
and then bootstrap to
get a nice trade off

593
00:30:25,690 --> 00:30:27,758
between bias and variance.

594
00:30:27,758 --> 00:30:29,800
And I think we'll probably
talk a little bit more

595
00:30:29,800 --> 00:30:31,880
about that next week.

596
00:30:31,880 --> 00:30:38,340
So I'll just highlight this
here that this has low bias

597
00:30:38,340 --> 00:30:42,040
and high variance on this one.

598
00:30:42,040 --> 00:30:44,650
So this one has low
variance and high bias.

599
00:30:44,650 --> 00:30:47,750
The other one is the opposite.

600
00:30:47,750 --> 00:30:49,840
All right, cool.

601
00:30:49,840 --> 00:30:52,250
So just to think
about this, so when

602
00:30:52,250 --> 00:30:54,390
we are thinking
about these targets,

603
00:30:54,390 --> 00:30:56,750
we can go between
these different ones.

604
00:30:56,750 --> 00:30:58,140
And then, oops, sorry.

605
00:30:58,140 --> 00:30:59,430
Somehow, these got copied.

606
00:30:59,430 --> 00:31:03,030
OK, so these are all things
that you can plug in.

607
00:31:03,030 --> 00:31:05,330
You can make different
choices over whether you

608
00:31:05,330 --> 00:31:08,862
plug in these n step
methods or others.

609
00:31:08,862 --> 00:31:11,570
And then you can use this all as
part of your actor critic policy

610
00:31:11,570 --> 00:31:12,910
gradient method.

611
00:31:12,910 --> 00:31:15,410
So now what we're going to do,
and I'll just make it a slide

612
00:31:15,410 --> 00:31:17,000
to delete those slides.

613
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:18,620
So now what we're
going to go into

614
00:31:18,620 --> 00:31:20,567
is more advanced policy
gradient methods.

615
00:31:20,567 --> 00:31:22,400
OK, so those are the
basic ones, and they're

616
00:31:22,400 --> 00:31:24,500
kind of the backbone between
all of the algorithms

617
00:31:24,500 --> 00:31:25,730
that we do now.

618
00:31:25,730 --> 00:31:27,500
But there's been
a lot of interest

619
00:31:27,500 --> 00:31:30,050
in these types of methods
and how do we scale them up,

620
00:31:30,050 --> 00:31:31,910
and how do we make them better.

621
00:31:31,910 --> 00:31:36,140
And we'll talk about what
we mean by better here.

622
00:31:36,140 --> 00:31:40,130
So actually, we'll probably talk
about some of this next week

623
00:31:40,130 --> 00:31:42,440
because I wanted to
make sure that we

624
00:31:42,440 --> 00:31:45,482
got through the algorithm
today so that you guys can have

625
00:31:45,482 --> 00:31:47,190
all the knowledge you
need to be starting

626
00:31:47,190 --> 00:31:48,447
to do the implementation.

627
00:31:48,447 --> 00:31:50,405
And then we'll do more
on the theory next week.

628
00:31:53,010 --> 00:31:54,450
So policy gradients,
so far, we've

629
00:31:54,450 --> 00:31:55,590
been talking about
them being great,

630
00:31:55,590 --> 00:31:57,423
and we know that they're
used in some really

631
00:31:57,423 --> 00:31:58,890
important application areas.

632
00:31:58,890 --> 00:32:02,460
Why do we have to go beyond
the methods that we just saw?

633
00:32:02,460 --> 00:32:06,480
Well, there's a couple of
different limitations to them.

634
00:32:06,480 --> 00:32:11,940
One is that the sample
efficiency is generally poor.

635
00:32:11,940 --> 00:32:17,430
And so in general, you
have to do many rollouts

636
00:32:17,430 --> 00:32:21,270
with the same policy in
order to get a good estimate

637
00:32:21,270 --> 00:32:21,960
of the gradient.

638
00:32:21,960 --> 00:32:23,320
Because you want a good
estimate of the gradient,

639
00:32:23,320 --> 00:32:24,987
because otherwise,
when you take a step,

640
00:32:24,987 --> 00:32:26,880
you might get somewhere
that's not as good

641
00:32:26,880 --> 00:32:29,700
or, you don't get to the
place you want to as quickly.

642
00:32:29,700 --> 00:32:32,220
And the other, and we're going
to see an example of this

643
00:32:32,220 --> 00:32:35,940
shortly, is that the distance
in the parameter space

644
00:32:35,940 --> 00:32:39,600
doesn't necessarily equal the
distance in the policy space.

645
00:32:39,600 --> 00:32:41,380
So this is a little
bit weird of an idea.

646
00:32:41,380 --> 00:32:44,290
But the idea is that, if you
have a policy parameterization,

647
00:32:44,290 --> 00:32:46,600
whether it's like a deep
neural network or others,

648
00:32:46,600 --> 00:32:48,280
there's some
parameters in there.

649
00:32:48,280 --> 00:32:50,830
And when you change them,
when you change your theta,

650
00:32:50,830 --> 00:32:52,810
you're going to get a
different policy out.

651
00:32:52,810 --> 00:32:56,530
But whether that is really
smooth, if I change theta,

652
00:32:56,530 --> 00:32:57,820
let's say theta is a scalar.

653
00:32:57,820 --> 00:32:59,410
So maybe theta is like 0.7.

654
00:32:59,410 --> 00:33:01,770
If I change it to
0.75, does that

655
00:33:01,770 --> 00:33:04,890
smoothly change how much more
I take a particular action,

656
00:33:04,890 --> 00:33:07,050
or might it be
really discontinuous?

657
00:33:07,050 --> 00:33:09,660
Might it suddenly say you
were taking this action

658
00:33:09,660 --> 00:33:12,330
with 20% probability, and
I changed it a little bit,

659
00:33:12,330 --> 00:33:16,410
and now I'm like taking that
action with 90% probability.

660
00:33:16,410 --> 00:33:17,850
The main idea
here, and we'll see

661
00:33:17,850 --> 00:33:22,570
an example of this in a second,
is this part may not be smooth,

662
00:33:22,570 --> 00:33:25,720
which means that even really
small changes in your theta,

663
00:33:25,720 --> 00:33:28,680
in your policy
parameterization, might actually

664
00:33:28,680 --> 00:33:32,197
lead to really big differences
in how you're making decisions.

665
00:33:32,197 --> 00:33:34,530
So you could imagine your
robot picks up things one way,

666
00:33:34,530 --> 00:33:36,363
and then you change
your theta a little bit,

667
00:33:36,363 --> 00:33:37,980
and suddenly, it
drives off a cliff.

668
00:33:37,980 --> 00:33:41,130
OK, not quite that
extreme, but it's

669
00:33:41,130 --> 00:33:44,903
not clear that, as we smoothly
take gradient steps in theta,

670
00:33:44,903 --> 00:33:46,320
if that's going
to smoothly change

671
00:33:46,320 --> 00:33:49,810
our policy parameterization
or policy decisions.

672
00:33:49,810 --> 00:33:52,050
So let's look at those both.

673
00:33:52,050 --> 00:33:52,600
All right.

674
00:33:52,600 --> 00:33:53,650
Sample efficiency.

675
00:33:53,650 --> 00:33:55,140
So what we've been
seeing so far is

676
00:33:55,140 --> 00:33:57,070
the idea is that
we take our policy,

677
00:33:57,070 --> 00:33:59,230
we roll it out
one or more times,

678
00:33:59,230 --> 00:34:01,110
and then we take
a gradient step,

679
00:34:01,110 --> 00:34:06,330
and we take one gradient step,
and then we roll out our policy

680
00:34:06,330 --> 00:34:07,740
again.

681
00:34:07,740 --> 00:34:09,330
And in general,
it would be really

682
00:34:09,330 --> 00:34:12,900
nice to be able to take
multiple gradient steps.

683
00:34:12,900 --> 00:34:15,090
But so far, we
have not seen that.

684
00:34:15,090 --> 00:34:17,639
And it's called on
policy expectation.

685
00:34:17,639 --> 00:34:22,153
So this is similar to
SARSA and other methods

686
00:34:22,153 --> 00:34:23,820
we've seen before,
where you're learning

687
00:34:23,820 --> 00:34:27,060
about a policy and its value
by actually executing it.

688
00:34:27,060 --> 00:34:30,929
So the problem is
when we think about--

689
00:34:30,929 --> 00:34:34,139
let me just go back to here.

690
00:34:34,139 --> 00:34:36,670
When we think about
doing these gradients,

691
00:34:36,670 --> 00:34:38,760
we've assumed that we've
gotten trajectories

692
00:34:38,760 --> 00:34:41,949
from the policy and the theta
that we're at right now.

693
00:34:41,949 --> 00:34:44,199
And then we use that
data to take a step.

694
00:34:44,199 --> 00:34:45,580
So now we're at some point.

695
00:34:45,580 --> 00:34:46,980
Here's our theta.

696
00:34:46,980 --> 00:34:50,280
We're at some point, and
we estimate the gradient

697
00:34:50,280 --> 00:34:51,840
from that point.

698
00:34:51,840 --> 00:34:54,780
So we estimate it
from trajectories

699
00:34:54,780 --> 00:34:58,590
that are generated under
theta, and then we take a step.

700
00:34:58,590 --> 00:35:04,300
Now, the problem is,
now we might be here.

701
00:35:04,300 --> 00:35:07,030
And what we would like to
do is to take another step

702
00:35:07,030 --> 00:35:08,600
before getting more data.

703
00:35:08,600 --> 00:35:10,570
But the problem
is, we don't have--

704
00:35:10,570 --> 00:35:12,430
let's call this theta prime.

705
00:35:12,430 --> 00:35:16,070
What we have is we
have data from theta.

706
00:35:16,070 --> 00:35:18,860
We don't have any
data from theta prime.

707
00:35:18,860 --> 00:35:20,960
So a priori, it
shouldn't be clear

708
00:35:20,960 --> 00:35:22,758
that we could take
more than one step.

709
00:35:22,758 --> 00:35:25,050
You can take one step because
we have data about theta.

710
00:35:25,050 --> 00:35:27,410
We estimate the
gradient at that point.

711
00:35:27,410 --> 00:35:29,662
Now, we would like to
just be able to continue

712
00:35:29,662 --> 00:35:31,370
to take gradient steps
before we actually

713
00:35:31,370 --> 00:35:33,360
go out in the real world
and gather more data.

714
00:35:33,360 --> 00:35:36,230
But it shouldn't be
obvious how to do that yet.

715
00:35:36,230 --> 00:35:39,118
And so when we talk about
policy gradient right now,

716
00:35:39,118 --> 00:35:40,910
we've been talking
about on policy methods,

717
00:35:40,910 --> 00:35:43,820
where we just try to estimate
the gradient for the policy we

718
00:35:43,820 --> 00:35:46,250
just executed.

719
00:35:46,250 --> 00:35:49,170
So similar to SARSA.

720
00:35:49,170 --> 00:35:52,945
Now, so what we would
like to be able to do is--

721
00:35:52,945 --> 00:35:54,570
so that's what we've
been doing so far.

722
00:35:54,570 --> 00:35:56,230
We collect sample
trajectories from the policy,

723
00:35:56,230 --> 00:35:57,610
then we form a sample estimate.

724
00:35:57,610 --> 00:35:58,510
It's pretty stable.

725
00:35:58,510 --> 00:36:01,590
We get our gradient, we take
a step, we rinse and repeat.

726
00:36:01,590 --> 00:36:04,080
Another thing we could do,
thinking about Q-learning

727
00:36:04,080 --> 00:36:05,910
or others is, well,
what if we could

728
00:36:05,910 --> 00:36:08,430
use that old data to
estimate the gradient

729
00:36:08,430 --> 00:36:10,480
at some other theta,
some other policy.

730
00:36:10,480 --> 00:36:11,670
Could we do that?

731
00:36:11,670 --> 00:36:14,670
This is known as off
policy estimates.

732
00:36:14,670 --> 00:36:17,020
This generally can start
to be pretty unstable.

733
00:36:17,020 --> 00:36:18,728
We're going to think
about different ways

734
00:36:18,728 --> 00:36:19,920
we could even do that.

735
00:36:19,920 --> 00:36:21,295
But we really would like that.

736
00:36:21,295 --> 00:36:23,170
We would like to be able
to use our old data,

737
00:36:23,170 --> 00:36:26,250
take multiple gradient
steps before we actually

738
00:36:26,250 --> 00:36:27,978
have to gather data.

739
00:36:27,978 --> 00:36:29,520
And you can imagine
that might end up

740
00:36:29,520 --> 00:36:31,603
allowing us to be much
more data efficient so

741
00:36:31,603 --> 00:36:34,020
that the total amount of times
we have to gather more data

742
00:36:34,020 --> 00:36:35,700
is much less.

743
00:36:35,700 --> 00:36:38,563
So we're going to think
about a way today,

744
00:36:38,563 --> 00:36:40,230
and we'll talk more
about this certainly

745
00:36:40,230 --> 00:36:41,650
over the next few
weeks, as well,

746
00:36:41,650 --> 00:36:43,770
of how do we use
our old data to,

747
00:36:43,770 --> 00:36:48,210
essentially, move faster
in our parameter space.

748
00:36:48,210 --> 00:36:50,460
Here's the second big challenge.

749
00:36:50,460 --> 00:36:53,430
So in general, we're going to
be doing stochastic gradient

750
00:36:53,430 --> 00:36:55,948
ascent with some step size.

751
00:36:55,948 --> 00:36:57,990
We've repeatedly thought
of there being some sort

752
00:36:57,990 --> 00:37:00,450
of learning rate or step size.

753
00:37:00,450 --> 00:37:02,250
One of the challenges--
and this was

754
00:37:02,250 --> 00:37:04,510
important for deep Q learning.

755
00:37:04,510 --> 00:37:06,732
We thought about it even
for TD learning and stuff.

756
00:37:06,732 --> 00:37:07,690
What was the step size?

757
00:37:07,690 --> 00:37:09,565
How much do we update
our estimate every time

758
00:37:09,565 --> 00:37:11,030
we get new data?

759
00:37:11,030 --> 00:37:13,040
Turns out it's much harder here.

760
00:37:13,040 --> 00:37:16,350
Now, we saw before that,
under some pretty loose,

761
00:37:16,350 --> 00:37:18,700
loose requirements
on the learning rate,

762
00:37:18,700 --> 00:37:21,730
we could, at least
in tabular cases,

763
00:37:21,730 --> 00:37:24,180
guarantee to
converge, et cetera.

764
00:37:24,180 --> 00:37:27,390
Policy gradient methods
are a little bit different.

765
00:37:27,390 --> 00:37:31,770
Here, the step size
really matters.

766
00:37:31,770 --> 00:37:36,190
And if we take a step size
that is really quite bad,

767
00:37:36,190 --> 00:37:39,510
we can collapse in
our performance.

768
00:37:39,510 --> 00:37:41,610
Does anybody have
an idea of why?

769
00:37:41,610 --> 00:37:44,870
Why does that happen?

770
00:37:44,870 --> 00:37:46,306
We why can we suddenly collapse?

771
00:37:51,490 --> 00:37:56,635
So I [INAUDIBLE] might
mix the optimal target,

772
00:37:56,635 --> 00:37:59,490
and then go for the win rate.

773
00:37:59,490 --> 00:38:00,660
Yeah, that is great.

774
00:38:00,660 --> 00:38:01,160
Great.

775
00:38:01,160 --> 00:38:03,190
So let's look at an example.

776
00:38:03,190 --> 00:38:05,480
So remember, the way that
we're getting our data

777
00:38:05,480 --> 00:38:06,513
is from our policy.

778
00:38:09,920 --> 00:38:12,412
So let's say we're trying
to get to this point.

779
00:38:12,412 --> 00:38:13,620
You have a big learning rate.

780
00:38:13,620 --> 00:38:15,500
So we took big steps.

781
00:38:15,500 --> 00:38:19,430
You might now get to part of
the space, which is really bad,

782
00:38:19,430 --> 00:38:20,910
really, really bad policies.

783
00:38:20,910 --> 00:38:23,285
By really bad policies, I mean
they have really bad value

784
00:38:23,285 --> 00:38:24,860
functions.

785
00:38:24,860 --> 00:38:28,280
If you have really bad value
functions and trajectories,

786
00:38:28,280 --> 00:38:30,140
which are visiting
states and actions, which

787
00:38:30,140 --> 00:38:32,450
all have really bad
reward, it's really hard

788
00:38:32,450 --> 00:38:35,210
to estimate a good
gradient of where to go.

789
00:38:35,210 --> 00:38:38,690
In general, you might be in
a really long plateau place.

790
00:38:38,690 --> 00:38:40,550
So this could be a really long.

791
00:38:40,550 --> 00:38:42,260
And so the gradient
here might be

792
00:38:42,260 --> 00:38:44,660
really hard to estimate
of how do I get back

793
00:38:44,660 --> 00:38:46,020
to that local optima.

794
00:38:46,020 --> 00:38:48,440
In general, it's not
going to be impossible,

795
00:38:48,440 --> 00:38:50,620
unless it's completely flat.

796
00:38:50,620 --> 00:38:54,780
But it might be really
close to completely flat.

797
00:38:54,780 --> 00:38:57,300
And so that's a big problem,
is that you don't necessarily

798
00:38:57,300 --> 00:39:00,405
know how large your
step size should be.

799
00:39:00,405 --> 00:39:03,030
On the other hand, if you use a
really small step sizes, that's

800
00:39:03,030 --> 00:39:06,427
bad, too, because each
time, your step size

801
00:39:06,427 --> 00:39:08,760
is basically determining how
much you change your policy

802
00:39:08,760 --> 00:39:10,030
before you get new data.

803
00:39:10,030 --> 00:39:13,230
And so you would like to take
as big a step sizes as possible

804
00:39:13,230 --> 00:39:16,740
that don't overstep and that
allow you to quickly get

805
00:39:16,740 --> 00:39:18,970
to the optimal local optima.

806
00:39:18,970 --> 00:39:21,390
Now, things like of
atom style optimizers,

807
00:39:21,390 --> 00:39:25,380
et cetera, help, but it won't
necessarily solve the problem.

808
00:39:25,380 --> 00:39:27,530
And one of the
challenges here is,

809
00:39:27,530 --> 00:39:30,480
we're only getting
information about the states

810
00:39:30,480 --> 00:39:33,005
and actions that are
visited under our policy.

811
00:39:33,005 --> 00:39:34,380
And you just might
get to regions

812
00:39:34,380 --> 00:39:36,240
where there's very
little information

813
00:39:36,240 --> 00:39:39,020
to estimate those gradients.

814
00:39:39,020 --> 00:39:40,350
Here's another challenge.

815
00:39:40,350 --> 00:39:46,080
And this relates to that we're
taking steps in the theta space,

816
00:39:46,080 --> 00:39:48,140
not directly in
terms of the actions

817
00:39:48,140 --> 00:39:50,510
we take when we
update our policy.

818
00:39:50,510 --> 00:39:54,050
So let's think about a
parameterization, which

819
00:39:54,050 --> 00:39:55,950
is a pretty simple
parameterization.

820
00:39:55,950 --> 00:39:58,255
So this is a logistic function.

821
00:40:03,950 --> 00:40:07,010
And this is 1 over 1
plus e to the minus w.

822
00:40:10,456 --> 00:40:14,130
So in this case, this
is the parameterization.

823
00:40:14,130 --> 00:40:15,660
It's kind of like a softmax.

824
00:40:15,660 --> 00:40:17,930
You just have some probability
of going to one action,

825
00:40:17,930 --> 00:40:20,060
and the rest of the
probability goes to the other,

826
00:40:20,060 --> 00:40:22,820
and you've parameterized
it with theta.

827
00:40:22,820 --> 00:40:26,630
So if theta is equal
to 0, it's 50/50.

828
00:40:26,630 --> 00:40:28,550
You'll either take a1 or a2.

829
00:40:28,550 --> 00:40:32,850
If theta is equal to 2, you
suddenly take a2 is much less.

830
00:40:32,850 --> 00:40:37,970
And if theta is equal to 4,
you basically never take a2.

831
00:40:37,970 --> 00:40:43,130
And that's just because of how
our relationship goes from theta

832
00:40:43,130 --> 00:40:45,175
to pi of a.

833
00:40:47,800 --> 00:40:50,240
In this parameterization,
it's pretty extreme.

834
00:40:50,240 --> 00:40:52,810
So as we make sort of small,
relatively small changes

835
00:40:52,810 --> 00:40:56,420
to theta, I didn't make
theta a million or anything.

836
00:40:56,420 --> 00:40:59,830
I've basically shifted,
even with 0, to 2, to 4,

837
00:40:59,830 --> 00:41:02,800
I've radically shifted how
much of my probability mass

838
00:41:02,800 --> 00:41:05,590
goes on to a1.

839
00:41:05,590 --> 00:41:08,595
And that's just to illustrate
this issue of smoothness

840
00:41:08,595 --> 00:41:10,720
that, as I make what might
be considered relatively

841
00:41:10,720 --> 00:41:12,520
small changes in my
theta space, that

842
00:41:12,520 --> 00:41:15,780
might make my policy
near deterministic.

843
00:41:15,780 --> 00:41:18,290
And we know that if our
policy is deterministic,

844
00:41:18,290 --> 00:41:21,852
we can't learn anything
about other actions.

845
00:41:21,852 --> 00:41:23,560
So let me just make
this a little smaller

846
00:41:23,560 --> 00:41:24,727
so you can see the question.

847
00:41:24,727 --> 00:41:28,450
So the challenge in this case
is that step size can matter

848
00:41:28,450 --> 00:41:30,710
a lot in terms of efficiency.

849
00:41:30,710 --> 00:41:32,960
We don't necessarily know
what the right step size is,

850
00:41:32,960 --> 00:41:34,480
and it may be hard
for us to know

851
00:41:34,480 --> 00:41:37,000
how small changes in
our parameter space

852
00:41:37,000 --> 00:41:39,700
relate to changes in
the action distributions

853
00:41:39,700 --> 00:41:40,900
we actually follow.

854
00:41:40,900 --> 00:41:42,670
And so what we'd
really like to do here

855
00:41:42,670 --> 00:41:45,100
is to actually come up
with an update rule that

856
00:41:45,100 --> 00:41:47,960
doesn't over-change
the policy too quickly,

857
00:41:47,960 --> 00:41:50,920
but still allows us to
make rapid progress.

858
00:41:50,920 --> 00:41:53,440
So we'd like to move as
far as we can in a way

859
00:41:53,440 --> 00:41:55,480
that we think is,
really, ideally,

860
00:41:55,480 --> 00:41:57,170
is going to just
directly increase

861
00:41:57,170 --> 00:41:58,170
the value of our policy.

862
00:42:00,665 --> 00:42:03,040
And I guess I'll say, well,
we'll see a bit more of that.

863
00:42:03,040 --> 00:42:06,148
And also, ideally, we would
like this all to be monotonic.

864
00:42:06,148 --> 00:42:08,440
We would like it so that if
we think back to the policy

865
00:42:08,440 --> 00:42:09,910
improvement
algorithms that we've

866
00:42:09,910 --> 00:42:13,390
seen before, policy improvement
algorithms for the tabular

867
00:42:13,390 --> 00:42:15,500
case where we knew
how the world worked

868
00:42:15,500 --> 00:42:17,650
had this great property
that every time we

869
00:42:17,650 --> 00:42:21,527
updated our policy, we got a
better policy or we were done.

870
00:42:21,527 --> 00:42:23,360
Now, the world is much
more complicated now.

871
00:42:23,360 --> 00:42:26,910
We've got these sort of
continuous parameterizations.

872
00:42:26,910 --> 00:42:29,190
We're not guaranteed to
get to the optimal policy,

873
00:42:29,190 --> 00:42:31,370
but it would be really
cool if we could still

874
00:42:31,370 --> 00:42:32,953
guarantee that we're
going to just get

875
00:42:32,953 --> 00:42:37,290
sort of monotonic improvement,
unless we get to a local optima.

876
00:42:37,290 --> 00:42:40,513
And the things that
I've shown you so far

877
00:42:40,513 --> 00:42:42,930
don't necessarily have that
property because they'll still

878
00:42:42,930 --> 00:42:44,760
converge to a local
optima, but you might

879
00:42:44,760 --> 00:42:46,770
overstep, like we see here.

880
00:42:46,770 --> 00:42:48,015
So you might go over.

881
00:42:48,015 --> 00:42:52,140
You might be having monotonic
improvement and then crash,

882
00:42:52,140 --> 00:42:54,130
and then you have to
go back and forth.

883
00:42:54,130 --> 00:42:56,887
So we have not guaranteed
monotonic improvement so far.

884
00:42:56,887 --> 00:42:58,470
But that would be
really nice and that

885
00:42:58,470 --> 00:43:00,553
could be important in a
lot of real world domains,

886
00:43:00,553 --> 00:43:01,740
like you'd imagine.

887
00:43:01,740 --> 00:43:04,373
If you were using this for
health care applications,

888
00:43:04,373 --> 00:43:06,540
you would really like to
have monotonic improvement,

889
00:43:06,540 --> 00:43:09,850
and not suddenly
performance crash.

890
00:43:09,850 --> 00:43:10,960
All right.

891
00:43:10,960 --> 00:43:13,997
So let's think about how we
might be able to get here.

892
00:43:13,997 --> 00:43:16,330
And you can think of a lot
of this lecture is motivating

893
00:43:16,330 --> 00:43:19,990
the things that you're going
to be doing in Homework 2,

894
00:43:19,990 --> 00:43:21,400
including the theory.

895
00:43:21,400 --> 00:43:23,140
So in general, what
we'd like to have is

896
00:43:23,140 --> 00:43:26,080
we'd like to have an update step
that uses all the data that we

897
00:43:26,080 --> 00:43:28,370
just got as efficiently
as possible,

898
00:43:28,370 --> 00:43:30,650
and that takes steps
that sort of respect

899
00:43:30,650 --> 00:43:34,720
this distance in the policy,
like the decision space, as

900
00:43:34,720 --> 00:43:38,545
opposed to just smoothness
in the parameter space.

901
00:43:38,545 --> 00:43:41,260
And in order to do that,
we need to understand

902
00:43:41,260 --> 00:43:44,560
how does the performance
of two policies relate.

903
00:43:44,560 --> 00:43:47,710
So we have data from one
policy, and we're considering

904
00:43:47,710 --> 00:43:49,670
trying to move to a new policy.

905
00:43:49,670 --> 00:43:51,730
And we'd really like to
know, OK, given the data

906
00:43:51,730 --> 00:43:53,260
that I have from
policy one, what

907
00:43:53,260 --> 00:43:55,900
does it tell me about how
good policy two might be?

908
00:43:55,900 --> 00:43:58,930
Because ideally,
policy one's data

909
00:43:58,930 --> 00:44:01,060
would allow us to tell us
which policy two I should

910
00:44:01,060 --> 00:44:02,930
move to next.

911
00:44:02,930 --> 00:44:05,960
So this is what you're
proving in Homework 2.

912
00:44:05,960 --> 00:44:09,010
You're going to prove the
performance difference lemma.

913
00:44:09,010 --> 00:44:10,690
And the performance
difference lemma,

914
00:44:10,690 --> 00:44:17,050
it allows us to relate the
performance of one policy

915
00:44:17,050 --> 00:44:19,960
to the performance of
another policy, given

916
00:44:19,960 --> 00:44:22,530
data from one of the policies.

917
00:44:22,530 --> 00:44:23,990
Let me just state this out.

918
00:44:23,990 --> 00:44:25,810
So what does this say?

919
00:44:25,810 --> 00:44:33,070
This is the value of one policy,
policy one, policy pi prime.

920
00:44:33,070 --> 00:44:36,340
I'm just using j
here, but this is

921
00:44:36,340 --> 00:44:39,190
value V. You can think
of this as just V.

922
00:44:39,190 --> 00:44:40,940
So what is that equal to?

923
00:44:40,940 --> 00:44:45,670
That is equal to the expectation
over trajectories that

924
00:44:45,670 --> 00:44:48,970
are sampled using pi prime.

925
00:44:48,970 --> 00:44:58,930
And again, if this is finite,
can use gamma equal to 1.

926
00:44:58,930 --> 00:45:02,740
We sum over the
distribution of trajectories

927
00:45:02,740 --> 00:45:08,530
you could get if you followed
policy pi 1 times the advantage

928
00:45:08,530 --> 00:45:12,460
under policy pi.

929
00:45:12,460 --> 00:45:19,000
So this part here is just equal
to the difference between if you

930
00:45:19,000 --> 00:45:24,700
took an action minus.

931
00:45:30,830 --> 00:45:34,250
But note here, because
this expectation here

932
00:45:34,250 --> 00:45:37,737
is over pi prime, the way
we're selecting these actions,

933
00:45:37,737 --> 00:45:39,445
I'll just write it
out a little bit more.

934
00:45:42,340 --> 00:45:46,090
Imagine we have
deterministic policies.

935
00:45:46,090 --> 00:45:48,430
So it's like we're
thinking about the Q value,

936
00:45:48,430 --> 00:45:51,880
if we first take an action
according to policy pi prime

937
00:45:51,880 --> 00:45:54,612
and then follow policy
pi for the rest of time,

938
00:45:54,612 --> 00:45:57,070
versus what we would have gotten
if we just followed policy

939
00:45:57,070 --> 00:45:58,300
pi the whole time.

940
00:45:58,300 --> 00:46:00,440
So you can think of it
as sort of breaking down

941
00:46:00,440 --> 00:46:02,440
the difference in the
value between two policies

942
00:46:02,440 --> 00:46:05,092
into a series of
small differences of,

943
00:46:05,092 --> 00:46:07,300
well, how much gain would
I have gotten at this state

944
00:46:07,300 --> 00:46:10,510
if I had taken pi prime's action
instead of the one I actually

945
00:46:10,510 --> 00:46:11,200
took.

946
00:46:11,200 --> 00:46:12,580
OK, what about here?

947
00:46:12,580 --> 00:46:15,160
And then you of want to
sum up all those additions.

948
00:46:15,160 --> 00:46:16,930
Every day, I'm
happier because I went

949
00:46:16,930 --> 00:46:18,222
to Stanford instead of Harvard.

950
00:46:18,222 --> 00:46:20,263
And I just add up all of
those, and that tells me

951
00:46:20,263 --> 00:46:22,440
over the course of my whole
career how much happier.

952
00:46:22,440 --> 00:46:25,990
I will have been,
hypothetically.

953
00:46:25,990 --> 00:46:29,183
OK, so this here is
over trajectories.

954
00:46:29,183 --> 00:46:30,850
Now we're going to
make a transformation

955
00:46:30,850 --> 00:46:34,300
and move it into state
action distributions.

956
00:46:34,300 --> 00:46:38,710
Because what this is
going to be here-- so now,

957
00:46:38,710 --> 00:46:40,340
this was over trajectories.

958
00:46:40,340 --> 00:46:44,450
We're going to rewrite this
just in terms of state action

959
00:46:44,450 --> 00:46:45,727
distributions.

960
00:46:45,727 --> 00:46:47,310
What we're going to
say is, all right,

961
00:46:47,310 --> 00:46:49,980
as we think about adding up
all these advantages, what

962
00:46:49,980 --> 00:46:51,980
I'm going to do is I'm
going to cluster together

963
00:46:51,980 --> 00:46:54,860
all the advantages that have
to do with the same state.

964
00:46:54,860 --> 00:46:57,350
So I think of there as
being a distribution

965
00:46:57,350 --> 00:47:00,320
over states I might
reach and actions I might

966
00:47:00,320 --> 00:47:02,570
take under policy pi prime.

967
00:47:02,570 --> 00:47:05,660
So if I just follow this policy,
I'm going to visit some states,

968
00:47:05,660 --> 00:47:07,610
and I'm just going
to think about what

969
00:47:07,610 --> 00:47:10,760
is the advantage in each
of those states weighed

970
00:47:10,760 --> 00:47:13,230
by how frequently I visit them.

971
00:47:13,230 --> 00:47:14,730
So we're sort of
transforming things

972
00:47:14,730 --> 00:47:17,010
from thinking of it as being
trajectories and thinking

973
00:47:17,010 --> 00:47:19,800
about weighting over
time steps, to weighting

974
00:47:19,800 --> 00:47:24,510
over a finite set or a
space of states and actions.

975
00:47:24,510 --> 00:47:26,050
And so we'll have
a distribution.

976
00:47:26,050 --> 00:47:29,050
It might be like, I visit
state 1 half the time.

977
00:47:29,050 --> 00:47:32,760
I visit state 7 only
1 in 10,000 times.

978
00:47:32,760 --> 00:47:37,590
So this allows us to
reweight the advantages.

979
00:47:37,590 --> 00:47:39,840
What does this
distribution look like?

980
00:47:39,840 --> 00:47:41,470
This looks like the following.

981
00:47:41,470 --> 00:47:43,565
Essentially, you just
think of what is my--

982
00:47:43,565 --> 00:47:45,690
so here, we're allowing us
to have discount factors

983
00:47:45,690 --> 00:47:47,523
because we're looking
for the infinite case.

984
00:47:47,523 --> 00:47:48,970
But you can adjust this.

985
00:47:48,970 --> 00:47:51,100
What this is just
saying is that, well,

986
00:47:51,100 --> 00:47:53,460
my weighted
distribution for state

987
00:47:53,460 --> 00:47:56,745
s is equal to, well, how
likely was I to be in state

988
00:47:56,745 --> 00:47:59,820
s on time step 1
under that policy.

989
00:47:59,820 --> 00:48:03,420
Well, how likely was I to
be in state s in time step

990
