What is KL divergence?

1233
00:59:22,440 --> 00:59:23,710
Some of you guys might
have seen this before,

1234
00:59:23,710 --> 00:59:25,650
but in case some
people might be new.

1235
00:59:25,650 --> 00:59:27,900
So what KL divergence
allows us to do

1236
00:59:27,900 --> 00:59:31,690
is to compare two
probability distributions.

1237
00:59:31,690 --> 00:59:36,180
So in our case, what this is
going to be is over actions

1238
00:59:36,180 --> 00:59:37,030
we will take.

1239
00:59:37,030 --> 00:59:41,460
So pi of a given s,
versus pi of a given s.

1240
00:59:41,460 --> 00:59:44,370
So both of these are
probability distributions

1241
00:59:44,370 --> 00:59:46,950
that sum to 1 for
a particular state.

1242
00:59:46,950 --> 00:59:50,400
And so in our case, what we
would be summing over here,

1243
00:59:50,400 --> 00:59:51,630
x would be a.

1244
00:59:51,630 --> 00:59:53,730
So we'd be summing
over all of these.

1245
00:59:53,730 --> 00:59:57,790
If you have the same
probability distribution,

1246
00:59:57,790 --> 00:59:59,050
the KL divergence is 0.

1247
00:59:59,050 --> 01:00:00,990
Otherwise, it's
strictly positive.

1248
01:00:00,990 --> 01:00:03,700
It's good to know
it's not symmetric

1249
01:00:03,700 --> 01:00:07,413
because we've made a choice
here in the ordering.

1250
01:00:07,413 --> 01:00:09,080
These are good
properties to know about.

1251
01:00:09,080 --> 01:00:13,150
It comes up all the time
in reinforcement learning.

1252
01:00:13,150 --> 01:00:16,630
So in our case, we can look
at, for a particular state s,

1253
01:00:16,630 --> 01:00:18,850
what is the KL divergence
and what the policies would

1254
01:00:18,850 --> 01:00:20,938
do for that particular state.

1255
01:00:20,938 --> 01:00:22,230
So that's what we've got there.

1256
01:00:22,230 --> 01:00:24,170
So it says, essentially,
how different

1257
01:00:24,170 --> 01:00:25,730
are the actions you would take.

1258
01:00:25,730 --> 01:00:27,080
Now, why is this good?

1259
01:00:27,080 --> 01:00:29,250
Well, we've been spending
some time saying, hey,

1260
01:00:29,250 --> 01:00:30,667
we really don't
want to think just

1261
01:00:30,667 --> 01:00:33,210
about how close we
are in theta space.

1262
01:00:33,210 --> 01:00:35,585
We really want to get to
thinking about how different are

1263
01:00:35,585 --> 01:00:37,700
our actual policies,
how different are

1264
01:00:37,700 --> 01:00:40,500
the actual decisions we
make in particular states.

1265
01:00:40,500 --> 01:00:42,530
And the nice thing
is, what this bound

1266
01:00:42,530 --> 01:00:47,630
says is that the difference
between two policies

1267
01:00:47,630 --> 01:00:51,180
is not just about how close you
are in some parameter space.

1268
01:00:51,180 --> 01:00:53,090
It's really about
how different are

1269
01:00:53,090 --> 01:00:55,220
the decisions you make
in all the states you'd

1270
01:00:55,220 --> 01:00:58,970
reach under your current
state distribution.

1271
01:00:58,970 --> 01:01:00,830
And so if you'd make
all the same decisions

1272
01:01:00,830 --> 01:01:02,610
in the states you're
already reaching,

1273
01:01:02,610 --> 01:01:05,220
your policy value is going
to be really similar.

1274
01:01:05,220 --> 01:01:07,380
If you would make very
different decisions,

1275
01:01:07,380 --> 01:01:09,578
then your policy value
might be really different,

1276
01:01:09,578 --> 01:01:11,870
because you'd go off and
explore really different parts

1277
01:01:11,870 --> 01:01:14,691
of the state space.

1278
01:01:14,691 --> 01:01:17,700
So this is really elegant.

1279
01:01:17,700 --> 01:01:22,440
And now, we have something where
we can just use our old data.

1280
01:01:22,440 --> 01:01:24,000
So we have our old data.

1281
01:01:24,000 --> 01:01:27,150
We can use it to estimate
what the performance

1282
01:01:27,150 --> 01:01:31,350
improvement would be if we
try to get to a new policy.

1283
01:01:31,350 --> 01:01:33,150
And so what you might
imagine in this case

1284
01:01:33,150 --> 01:01:35,460
is you could use it to
search over or decide

1285
01:01:35,460 --> 01:01:37,860
which new policy to try.

1286
01:01:37,860 --> 01:01:40,835
This allows you to compute it
for lots of different pi prime.

1287
01:01:40,835 --> 01:01:44,370
It doesn't just have to be the
pi prime for one gradient step.

1288
01:01:44,370 --> 01:01:47,280
It says, in general, even
outside of policy gradient

1289
01:01:47,280 --> 01:01:52,530
methods, you can evaluate the
value of changing your policy

1290
01:01:52,530 --> 01:01:55,170
to pi prime with respect
to your current performance

1291
01:01:55,170 --> 01:01:56,890
by this expression.

1292
01:01:56,890 --> 01:01:58,380
And this will be
more or less tight

1293
01:01:58,380 --> 01:02:00,690
depending on how
different your policy is

1294
01:02:00,690 --> 01:02:04,920
at making decisions in states
that you would currently reach.

1295
01:02:04,920 --> 01:02:06,320
And we'll talk more about this.

1296
01:02:06,320 --> 01:02:11,850
We haven't talked about this
yet, so we will talk about--

1297
01:02:18,182 --> 01:02:22,230
This also relates to some really
nice literature from the last 20

1298
01:02:22,230 --> 01:02:25,530
years of thinking about how
do we do monotonic policy

1299
01:02:25,530 --> 01:02:28,980
improvement in policy gradient
methods and policy search

1300
01:02:28,980 --> 01:02:29,740
methods.

1301
01:02:29,740 --> 01:02:32,310
It also relates to the notion
of a trust region, which

1302
01:02:32,310 --> 01:02:36,485
is this idea of, when
you're changing your policy,

1303
01:02:36,485 --> 01:02:37,860
how far can you
go and still sort

1304
01:02:37,860 --> 01:02:39,540
of trust the performance
of it and trust

1305
01:02:39,540 --> 01:02:40,710
you can get improvement.

1306
01:02:40,710 --> 01:02:43,330
So there's a bunch of different
nice papers related to this.

1307
01:02:43,330 --> 01:02:46,100
OK, let's talk
about the algorithm.

1308
01:02:46,100 --> 01:02:48,170
Proximal policy
optimization is going

1309
01:02:48,170 --> 01:02:50,670
to be inspired by all the things
that we just talked about.

1310
01:02:50,670 --> 01:02:52,280
So what we want
to do is, it wants

1311
01:02:52,280 --> 01:02:55,100
to be able to take
multiple gradient steps,

1312
01:02:55,100 --> 01:02:56,960
and it wants to be able
to do this in a way

1313
01:02:56,960 --> 01:02:59,110
so that we don't overstep.

1314
01:02:59,110 --> 01:03:03,290
We try to focus on policy
parameterization in terms

1315
01:03:03,290 --> 01:03:05,180
of the actual
decisions that we make.

1316
01:03:05,180 --> 01:03:07,700
So there are two
different variants.

1317
01:03:07,700 --> 01:03:10,850
One is it solves an
unconstrained optimization

1318
01:03:10,850 --> 01:03:14,390
problem where it uses
this approximation.

1319
01:03:14,390 --> 01:03:17,760
So that's the approximation
we had on the previous slides.

1320
01:03:17,760 --> 01:03:23,420
I'll just write down, from
prior slides, where we use data

1321
01:03:23,420 --> 01:03:27,200
from the current policy, and we
add up these weighted advantage

1322
01:03:27,200 --> 01:03:28,170
functions.

1323
01:03:28,170 --> 01:03:30,290
So what it says is, well,
the thing you want to do

1324
01:03:30,290 --> 01:03:33,530
is you want to pick the policy
that maximizes our estimated

1325
01:03:33,530 --> 01:03:37,560
difference, subject to a
constraint on the KL divergence.

1326
01:03:37,560 --> 01:03:41,300
Because it's realizing
that L approximation is

1327
01:03:41,300 --> 01:03:43,490
going to get worse and
worse as the KL divergence

1328
01:03:43,490 --> 01:03:44,700
gets really large.

1329
01:03:44,700 --> 01:03:54,520
So it's directly
incorporating this bound.

1330
01:03:54,520 --> 01:03:57,222
So it's thinking, OK, I want
to think about what this is,

1331
01:03:57,222 --> 01:03:58,930
but then I also have
to consider the fact

1332
01:03:58,930 --> 01:04:00,910
that my estimate might
be off by as much

1333
01:04:00,910 --> 01:04:04,670
as this square root of KL.

1334
01:04:04,670 --> 01:04:07,490
So you really want to improve
with respect to something

1335
01:04:07,490 --> 01:04:09,770
that considers both of those.

1336
01:04:09,770 --> 01:04:12,510
So this is one
version of policy.

1337
01:04:12,510 --> 01:04:15,620
This is not the way
most people do PPO.

1338
01:04:15,620 --> 01:04:17,250
We'll see the other
really common one.

1339
01:04:17,250 --> 01:04:19,160
But it's a nice
baseline to know about.

1340
01:04:19,160 --> 01:04:21,230
And here, when we
think about what

1341
01:04:21,230 --> 01:04:24,330
that KL is, as you
might have noticed,

1342
01:04:24,330 --> 01:04:27,170
KL is defined for
a single state.

1343
01:04:27,170 --> 01:04:29,030
So for a single
state, we can say

1344
01:04:29,030 --> 01:04:30,875
what is the distribution
over actions I take

1345
01:04:30,875 --> 01:04:32,820
in one policy versus another.

1346
01:04:32,820 --> 01:04:34,620
But of course, we
have lots of states.

1347
01:04:34,620 --> 01:04:36,260
And so what we can
do here is we can

1348
01:04:36,260 --> 01:04:40,830
take an expectation over the KL,
over all the states we visit.

1349
01:04:40,830 --> 01:04:43,940
And that was part of the
theoretical bound, too.

1350
01:04:43,940 --> 01:04:46,070
Another really important
thing you can see here

1351
01:04:46,070 --> 01:04:52,210
is this waiting between
trying to optimize this policy

1352
01:04:52,210 --> 01:04:56,230
improvement, while
respecting this KL penalty.

1353
01:04:56,230 --> 01:04:59,890
And you can change this
over each iteration

1354
01:04:59,890 --> 01:05:03,880
to approximately satisfy the
KL divergence constraint.

1355
01:05:03,880 --> 01:05:06,670
So this does not
guarantee that you will.

1356
01:05:06,670 --> 01:05:08,230
This does not
guarantee that you're

1357
01:05:08,230 --> 01:05:09,830
going to get
monotonic improvement,

1358
01:05:09,830 --> 01:05:12,893
but it's trying to
get towards that.

1359
01:05:12,893 --> 01:05:14,060
So let's see how that works.

1360
01:05:14,060 --> 01:05:15,992
So this is the algorithm.

1361
01:05:15,992 --> 01:05:17,950
What it does is, you can
compute the advantages

1362
01:05:17,950 --> 01:05:20,180
using any advantage
estimation algorithm.

1363
01:05:20,180 --> 01:05:22,540
You compute your
policy update, and you

1364
01:05:22,540 --> 01:05:24,330
can do K steps with that.

1365
01:05:24,330 --> 01:05:28,670
So the nice thing is that you
can use your old data here,

1366
01:05:28,670 --> 01:05:33,010
and you can take
multiple gradient steps.

1367
01:05:33,010 --> 01:05:35,140
After you do this,
you can also check

1368
01:05:35,140 --> 01:05:39,590
if your KL divergence for your
new resulting policy is large.

1369
01:05:39,590 --> 01:05:42,410
If it is, then you may
increase the penalty.

1370
01:05:42,410 --> 01:05:46,030
If it's small, you can
decrease the penalty.

1371
01:05:46,030 --> 01:05:48,020
And that just allows
us to trade off

1372
01:05:48,020 --> 01:05:50,630
between how much we pay
attention to this KL divergence

1373
01:05:50,630 --> 01:05:54,110
constraint versus not.

1374
01:05:54,110 --> 01:05:57,930
And as I noted here, you might
violate the KL constraints,

1375
01:05:57,930 --> 01:06:01,100
but most of them, they
don't, empirically.

1376
01:06:01,100 --> 01:06:03,710
This is one reasonable thing
to do based on everything

1377
01:06:03,710 --> 01:06:04,370
we've seen.

1378
01:06:04,370 --> 01:06:06,203
Now we're going to see
something else, which

1379
01:06:06,203 --> 01:06:08,870
is inspired by that is a
much more common thing to do,

1380
01:06:08,870 --> 01:06:11,400
which is, well, let me
just highlight here.

1381
01:06:11,400 --> 01:06:14,400
Multiple gradient
steps is really good.

1382
01:06:14,400 --> 01:06:17,240
So one of the benefits
is that we're not just

1383
01:06:17,240 --> 01:06:18,660
taking a single gradient step.

1384
01:06:18,660 --> 01:06:19,620
We're taking multiple.

1385
01:06:19,620 --> 01:06:21,710
So just to really
highlight that.

1386
01:06:21,710 --> 01:06:22,290
All right.

1387
01:06:22,290 --> 01:06:24,860
What is the other thing
we want to do here?

1388
01:06:24,860 --> 01:06:27,590
We haven't talked about
natural gradients.

1389
01:06:27,590 --> 01:06:30,140
But for any of you that
are familiar with these,

1390
01:06:30,140 --> 01:06:31,790
they're another
way to try to think

1391
01:06:31,790 --> 01:06:33,630
about taking gradient steps.

1392
01:06:33,630 --> 01:06:35,630
We're not going to talk
about that for now.

1393
01:06:35,630 --> 01:06:39,030
So the other thing we could
do is equipped objective.

1394
01:06:39,030 --> 01:06:42,320
So what we're going to
look at in this case is,

1395
01:06:42,320 --> 01:06:44,810
remember how we talked about
we had this kind of ratio

1396
01:06:44,810 --> 01:06:47,820
between this is what we're
using to weight our advantage

1397
01:06:47,820 --> 01:06:50,480
function, was the difference
between how likely

1398
01:06:50,480 --> 01:06:52,970
you were to take that
action under our old policy

1399
01:06:52,970 --> 01:06:53,920
versus our new.

1400
01:06:53,920 --> 01:06:57,720
And we're using it to weight
our advantage function.

1401
01:06:57,720 --> 01:06:59,710
What the clipping
does is it says,

1402
01:06:59,710 --> 01:07:04,780
well, I don't want this to
get too high or too low.

1403
01:07:04,780 --> 01:07:05,290
OK?

1404
01:07:05,290 --> 01:07:07,082
This could become really
high or really low

1405
01:07:07,082 --> 01:07:10,490
when my policies are
really different.

1406
01:07:10,490 --> 01:07:13,010
If my policy is really--

1407
01:07:13,010 --> 01:07:16,700
you can imagine that,
if my policy puts

1408
01:07:16,700 --> 01:07:18,560
really low probability
on something

1409
01:07:18,560 --> 01:07:21,200
that the current policy
puts high probability on,

1410
01:07:21,200 --> 01:07:24,680
this ratio here is going
to go towards-- this r is

1411
01:07:24,680 --> 01:07:27,570
going to go to about 0.

1412
01:07:27,570 --> 01:07:31,080
And if this puts very
high, let's say this is 1,

1413
01:07:31,080 --> 01:07:33,910
and this puts very low
probability on that,

1414
01:07:33,910 --> 01:07:35,440
this could be extremely large.

1415
01:07:35,440 --> 01:07:37,580
This could be like 10 to the 6.

1416
01:07:37,580 --> 01:07:39,540
It could be very, very large.

1417
01:07:39,540 --> 01:07:42,655
And both of those are being
used to weight the advantage

1418
01:07:42,655 --> 01:07:43,155
function.

1419
01:07:43,155 --> 01:07:43,470
Right?

1420
01:07:43,470 --> 01:07:46,012
So your advantage function could
be getting shrunk towards 0,

1421
01:07:46,012 --> 01:07:49,330
or it could be getting blown up
by a factor of, say, 10 to the 6

1422
01:07:49,330 --> 01:07:51,960
if you have a big difference
in the actions you

1423
01:07:51,960 --> 01:07:54,120
would take under one
policy versus another.

1424
01:07:54,120 --> 01:07:55,620
And in general, we
don't like things

1425
01:07:55,620 --> 01:07:57,960
where we're thinking of
policy gradients, where

1426
01:07:57,960 --> 01:08:02,010
we might have terms that
are exploding or vanishing.

1427
01:08:02,010 --> 01:08:04,560
And that's part of the point
of the KL divergence constraint

1428
01:08:04,560 --> 01:08:07,280
is to say, you want your
policies to stay close.

1429
01:08:07,280 --> 01:08:10,023
So the clipping is sort of
inspired by this general idea,

1430
01:08:10,023 --> 01:08:11,940
but says, well, maybe
something similar we can

1431
01:08:11,940 --> 01:08:13,290
is we're just going to clip.

1432
01:08:13,290 --> 01:08:16,710
We're just going to say you
can't have weighted advantage

1433
01:08:16,710 --> 01:08:20,670
terms that are going towards
infinity, or minus infinity,

1434
01:08:20,670 --> 01:08:22,350
or 0.

1435
01:08:22,350 --> 01:08:25,146
And so if this ratio
is too extreme,

1436
01:08:25,146 --> 01:08:26,229
I'm just going to clip it.

1437
01:08:26,229 --> 01:08:30,399
I'm going to not allow it to
be less than 1 minus epsilon,

1438
01:08:30,399 --> 01:08:33,250
or greater than 1 plus epsilon.

1439
01:08:33,250 --> 01:08:35,680
And epsilon is just
a hyperparameter.

1440
01:08:35,680 --> 01:08:37,810
And essentially,
it's sort of meaning

1441
01:08:37,810 --> 01:08:40,790
that your policy might
change further than that,

1442
01:08:40,790 --> 01:08:43,990
but that's not going to
benefit your loss function.

1443
01:08:43,990 --> 01:08:47,319
So this, again, is going to
constrain your policy class

1444
01:08:47,319 --> 01:08:50,979
to stay within this
region, for which it's

1445
01:08:50,979 --> 01:08:53,029
making similar decisions.

1446
01:08:53,029 --> 01:08:54,910
So we're still really
focusing on what

1447
01:08:54,910 --> 01:08:56,359
actions are we actually taking.

1448
01:08:56,359 --> 01:08:58,390
Are we taking similar
actions in these states,

1449
01:08:58,390 --> 01:09:01,390
as we would be normally,
regardless of how much

1450
01:09:01,390 --> 01:09:03,510
my theta is changing?

1451
01:09:03,510 --> 01:09:05,439
And then you just do
your policy update

1452
01:09:05,439 --> 01:09:07,490
by taking an argmax over this.

1453
01:09:07,490 --> 01:09:11,069
So this is your clipped
objective function.

1454
01:09:11,069 --> 01:09:11,569
All right.

1455
01:09:11,569 --> 01:09:13,750
So let's see how this works.

1456
01:09:13,750 --> 01:09:15,790
Let's think about
what it's doing.

1457
01:09:15,790 --> 01:09:19,630
So we'll do a quick
Check Your Understanding.

1458
01:09:19,630 --> 01:09:23,390
So this shows you
what L clip does.

1459
01:09:23,390 --> 01:09:25,569
This is L clip, as well.

1460
01:09:25,569 --> 01:09:27,640
And what I'd like
you to think about

1461
01:09:27,640 --> 01:09:31,090
is, what does this look like,
depending on the advantage

1462
01:09:31,090 --> 01:09:32,660
function.

1463
01:09:32,660 --> 01:09:35,710
So L. Let me just write it down.

1464
01:09:35,710 --> 01:09:37,970
L clip.

1465
01:09:37,970 --> 01:09:40,399
OK, so this is r.

1466
01:09:40,399 --> 01:09:42,800
So on the x-axis is r.

1467
01:09:42,800 --> 01:09:44,908
And then on the
y-axis is L clip.

1468
01:09:44,908 --> 01:09:46,700
And what this is asking
you to think about,

1469
01:09:46,700 --> 01:09:51,500
this is from their paper, is to
think about what does clipping

1470
01:09:51,500 --> 01:09:55,820
do in terms of your loss.

1471
01:09:55,820 --> 01:09:57,590
And so I'd like
you to think about,

1472
01:09:57,590 --> 01:10:02,360
in this case, which of
these two, if either,

1473
01:10:02,360 --> 01:10:04,850
match within the advantage
function as positive

1474
01:10:04,850 --> 01:10:07,320
or the advantage
